  "narrative": "The differential metabolomics profile is anchored by a single, overwhelmingly significant pathway. **Androgen and Estrogen Metabolism** (KEGG map00150; SMP00068) achieved an FDR of 0.0 with a fold-enrichment of 1501, matching six of the eight input metabolites: **estradiol** (C00951), **androstenedione** (C00280), **estrone** (C00468), **dehydroepiandrosterone** (C01227), **dihydrotestosterone** (C03917), and **testosterone** (C00535). The matched metabolites span the entire steroidogenic axis, from adrenal androgen precursors through to active sex steroids in both the androgenic and estrogenic arms.\n\n**Driver compounds and directional relationships.** KEGG graph traversal confirms a two-step biosynthetic cascade from **DHEA** (C01227) → **androstenedione** (C00280) → **testosterone** (C00535). Within the estrogenic arm, **estradiol** (C00951) and **estrone** (C00468) are connected by a single reversible step via 17β-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase. This architecture indicates that the treatment condition is perturbing the coordinated flux between the androgen and estrogen pools rather than a single isolated step. Dihydrotestosterone (C03917), the potent androgen receptor agonist, reflects downstream activation of the 5α-reductase arm.\n\nWarfarin (C01541) and 3,4-dihydroxyphenylacetaldehyde (C04043, DOPAL) did not enrich in the steroid pathways, suggesting they may reflect off-target pharmacological effects (CYP2C9 inhibition context for warfarin) or peripheral catecholamine metabolism, respectively.\n\n**Biological significance.** Broad dysregulation of the steroid metabolome is consistent with perturbation of CYP17A1, 3β-HSD, 17β-HSD, or aromatase activity — enzymes that sit at the nexus of androgen–estrogen interconversion. Such a signature could arise from pharmaceutical inhibition, endocrine-disrupting exposure, or a stress-mediated shift in the 17,20-lyase/17β-HSD ratio. The coexistence of elevated precursors (DHEA, androstenedione) alongside both androgenic (testosterone, DHT) and estrogenic (estradiol, estrone) endpoints suggests the block or surge is not confined to a single enzymatic step but affects the entire pathway simultaneously.",
